<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">Hålogaland</h1><p class="st-meta">700 CE – 900 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The far northern chieftaincy of Viking Age Norway, home of the traveller Ohthere and of powerful lords who traded furs with the Sámi.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Hålogaland stretched along the Norwegian coast north of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/earldom-of-lade">Trøndelag</a>, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lofoten">Lofoten</a> islands. Its chieftains grew rich in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>: the great longhouse at Borg in Lofoten, over 80 metres long, was the seat of a local lord from the 6th century.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Hålogaland lords controlled trade in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a>, walrus <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, hides and dried fish, much of it taken as tribute from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sami-people">Sámi</a>. Around 890 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohthere-of-halogaland">Ohthere</a>, a Hålogaland chieftain, described his life and his voyage round the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-cape">North Cape</a> to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-the-great">Alfred of Wessex</a>, the earliest written account of the region. Power rested on chieftains rather than a formal kingdom.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the late 9th century the region came under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harald-fairhair">Harald Fairhair</a> and the earls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lade-trondheim">Lade</a>, whose family is said to have come from Hålogaland. Local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnates</a> such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tore-hund">Tore Hund</a> remained powerful into the 11th century, before the area was fully integrated into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-norway">Kingdom of Norway</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/halogaland.jpg" alt="Hålogaland" width="320" height="413"><figcaption>Image: Drieakko · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Haalogaland_kart.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>700 CE – 900 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Norse paganism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Norse</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/norway">Norway</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Halogaland, Kingdom of Hålogaland</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=800&amp;focus=halogaland" aria-label="Hålogaland on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4952 7 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Hålogaland lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5272 263L5225 425"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5225 425h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5272 263h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5272 263h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=800&amp;focus=halogaland">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
